      "note_id": "shared_with_german",
      "kind": "helpful_transfer",
      "text": "English and German are close cousins: both are West Germanic, so many everyday words come from the same old ancestor and still look or sound alike. Some are nearly twins (Hand, Finger, Winter), some need a small sound shift (Wasser, Apfel, Vater, Salz, Nacht). The last four are German words that English borrowed whole.",
